Q) If most DBMS systems have similar features, why do companies use different DBMS technologies?
  - Because they differ in performance, scalability, security, cost, and support.

Q) Why might a company choose one DBMS over another?
  - To meet its specific business and technical needs.

Q) Why do companies migrate from one DBMS to another?
  - To improve performance, reduce costs, gain new features, or move to the cloud.

Q) What is database migration?
  - Moving data and applications from one DBMS to another.

Q) What technical challenges can occur during migration?
  - Data loss, compatibility issues, downtime, and application changes.

Q) What business challenges can occur during migration?
  - High costs, employee training, project delays, and business disruption.

Q) Why are migration projects considered high-risk?
  - Because failures can affect important business operations and data.

Q) What happens if data is lost during migration?
  - The company may lose important information and face financial losses.

Q) Why is testing important during migration?
  - To ensure the new system works correctly and data remains accurate.

Q) How can downtime affect a business during migration?

  - It can interrupt services and reduce productivity.

Q) How do database architecture decisions affect long-term systems?
  - They influence performance, scalability, maintenance, and future growth.

Q) Why is choosing the right DBMS important?

  - It helps ensure the system remains efficient and reliable over time.

Q) Can changing a DBMS be expensive?
  - Yes, because it may require new software, training, and migration efforts.

Q) Why do companies move from on-premises databases to cloud databases?
  - For better scalability, flexibility, and lower maintenance costs.

Q) What is the main lesson about DBMS selection and migration?
  - The right database choice supports business goals, while poor choices can lead to costly migrations later.